  "bdHelpTooltip": "What's this?",
  "bdHelpSectionWord": "The word",
  "bdHelpSectionInGit": "In git",
  "bdHelpSectionWhy": "Why and when",
  "bdHelpSectionLinks": "Learn more",
  "bdHelpCaptionBefore": "Before",
  "bdHelpCaptionAfter": "After",
  "bdHelpCaptionAfterFetch": "After fetch — your main hasn't moved",
  "bdHelpCaptionAfterPull": "After pull — your main catches up",
  "bdHelpCaptionAheadOne": "feature is one commit ahead of origin/feature (↑1)",
  "bdHelpLinkBranchingBasics": "Pro Git — Basic Branching and Merging",
  "bdHelpLinkRemoteBranches": "Pro Git — Remote Branches",
  "bdHelpLinkRebasing": "Pro Git — Rebasing",
  "bdHelpFetchVsPullTitle": "Fetch vs. pull",
  "bdHelpFetchVsPullWord": "To fetch is to go and get something; to pull is to draw it toward you.",
  "bdHelpFetchVsPullInGit": "Fetch downloads new commits from the remote (GitHub) and only moves the remote labels such as origin/main. Your branch and files stay as they are. Pull does a fetch and then joins those commits into your branch.",
  "bdHelpFetchVsPullWhy": "Fetch when you want to see what changed first; it's always safe. Pull when you want to catch up right away. Pull can stop if you have uncommitted changes, so commit first.",
  "bdHelpUpstreamTitle": "Upstream and origin",
  "bdHelpUpstreamWord": "Upstream is the part of a river the water comes from. Origin is where something comes from.",
  "bdHelpUpstreamInGit": "origin is the default name for the remote you cloned from, usually GitHub. The remote branch your branch is paired with (such as origin/feature) is its upstream. The ↑↓ numbers compare your branch with it.",
  "bdHelpUpstreamWhy": "With an upstream, plain Push and Pull know where to go. A new branch has none, so the first push is a Publish (git push -u), which sets it.",
  "bdHelpFastForwardTitle": "Fast-forward",
  "bdHelpFastForwardWord": "Winding a tape or video quickly forward.",
  "bdHelpFastForwardInGit": "When your branch is simply behind and hasn't split off, git makes no new commit; it just moves the branch label forward to the latest commit. It only winds forward along commits that already exist.",
  "bdHelpFastForwardWhy": "History stays in one clean line and there's nothing to conflict. If both sides have different new commits (history has split) it can't wind forward, so fast-forward only stops and you need a merge or rebase. A push rejected as non-fast-forward fails for the same reason.",
  "bdHelpMergeCommitTitle": "Merge commit",
  "bdHelpMergeCommitWord": "To merge is to join; two roads becoming one.",
  "bdHelpMergeCommitInGit": "Keeps both lines of history as they are and adds one new commit whose parents are both of them. The history shows exactly what was joined and when.",
  "bdHelpMergeCommitWhy": "It never rewrites commits you've already shared, so it's the safest. Good for branches you share with others. The trade-off is extra merge commits in the history.",
  "bdHelpRebaseTitle": "Rebase",
  "bdHelpRebaseWord": "Base is what something stands on; to rebase is to set it on a new base.",
  "bdHelpRebaseInGit": "Takes your commits off and replays them one by one on top of the commits you just got. The changes are the same, but they become new commits (C', D') and the history is a single line.",
  "bdHelpRebaseWhy": "Keeps history clean with no merge commits. Use it only on commits you haven't pushed yet: rebasing pushed commits makes your history differ from everyone else's and needs a force push.",
