  "bdHelpCaptionAfterRevert": "C stays; a new commit C⁻ undoes it",
  "bdHelpCaptionAfterCherryPick": "The change from feature's D is copied onto main as a new commit D'",
  "bdHelpRevertTitle": "Revert",
  "bdHelpRevertWord": "To revert is to go back to how something was.",
  "bdHelpRevertInGit": "Doesn't delete the commit — it adds a new commit that applies the opposite change. Both the original and the revert stay in history.",
  "bdHelpRevertWhy": "Use it when a commit you've already pushed causes trouble. History isn't rewritten, so nobody else is affected and no force push is needed. For commits not yet pushed, amending is cleaner.",
  "bdHelpCaptionBeforeReset": "Before: main points at D",
  "bdHelpCaptionAfterReset": "After: main moved to B. C and D look gone but are still in the reflog",
  "bdHelpCaptionReflog": "The places HEAD has been are listed in order, so you can go back to D",
  "bdHelpResetTitle": "Reset",
  "bdHelpResetWord": "To reset is to put something back where it was.",
  "bdHelpResetInGit": "Moves the branch back to an earlier commit. The commits look deleted but stay in the reflog for a while. --soft keeps the changes staged, --keep protects your uncommitted work, and --hard throws that work away too.",
  "bdHelpResetWhy": "Use it to undo commits you haven't pushed yet. Don't use it on pushed commits: it disagrees with everyone else's history, so use revert. This app only uses the safe --soft and --keep.",
  "bdHelpReflogTitle": "Reflog",
  "bdHelpReflogWord": "Reflog is short for 'reference log': a record of where a name pointed.",
  "bdHelpReflogInGit": "A record, kept only on this computer, of where HEAD and your branches pointed. Even after you undo a commit or delete a branch, you can usually find that point again for about 90 days.",
  "bdHelpReflogWhy": "When you think 'I made a mistake', look at the reflog first. It exists only on this computer, not on the remote or another machine, and git cleans up old entries.",
  "bdHistoryGraph": "Graph",
  "bdHistoryAllBranches": "All branches",
  "bdHelpCaptionGraphMerged": "main and feature split apart and meet again at M (the hollow dot is a merge commit)",
  "bdHelpCommitGraphTitle": "Reading the commit graph",
  "bdHelpCommitGraphWord": "A graph is a picture made of dots and lines.",
  "bdHelpCommitGraphInGit": "Dots are commits and lines connect each commit to its parent. Newest is at the top. A line that splits is where a branch began; two lines that join are a merge. A hollow dot is a merge commit, and a dot with a ring is where you are now (HEAD).",
  "bdHelpCommitGraphWhy": "See at a glance where your branch left main and where merges happened. In a narrow window only six lines are drawn; any more overlap on the right-most one.",
  "bdRecoveryTitle": "Undo a mistake",
  "bdRecoveryIntro": "Most mistakes can be undone. Pick what fits.",
